WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.754
OPC technologies help industrial applications exchange data across products and vendors.

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:08.139
Instead of every application implementing every device-specific interface,

00:00:08.139 --> 00:00:11.947
an OPC layer can expose process values, alarms, events,

00:00:11.947 --> 00:00:14.909
and other information through a common model.

00:00:14.909 --> 00:00:20.410
OPC Classic refers to older specifications built around Microsoft COM and DCOM technologies.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:24.641
These deployments may depend heavily on Windows configuration, service identities,

00:00:24.641 --> 00:00:25.911
and network permissions.

00:00:25.911 --> 00:00:29.296
Their architecture can be difficult to segment cleanly,

00:00:29.296 --> 00:00:32.681
so inventory and supported hardening guidance are important.

00:00:32.681 --> 00:00:36.489
OPC Unified Architecture, or OPC UA, is a newer,

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:39.027
platform-independent approach with richer information modeling.

00:00:39.027 --> 00:00:42.412
It can support client-server communication and publish-subscribe patterns.

00:00:42.412 --> 00:00:47.067
OPC UA also includes modern security capabilities such as application authentication,

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:48.759
user authentication, message signing,

00:00:48.759 --> 00:00:52.567
and encryption when profiles and endpoints are configured appropriately.

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:56.376
Available security is not the same as enabled security.

00:00:56.376 --> 00:00:58.914
Administrators must select suitable security policies,

00:00:58.914 --> 00:01:02.722
restrict anonymous or overly broad access, protect private keys,

00:01:02.722 --> 00:01:05.684
and manage application certificates and trust lists.

00:01:05.684 --> 00:01:09.069
Expired certificates, copied trust stores, weak fallback endpoints,

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:12.878
or inaccurate system time can undermine otherwise strong design.

00:01:12.878 --> 00:01:18.378
OPC servers and gateways often aggregate valuable data and connect multiple architectural levels.

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:22.609
Treat them as important assets: minimize services, separate management access,

00:01:22.609 --> 00:01:25.994
monitor configuration and trust changes, limit approved clients,

00:01:25.994 --> 00:01:29.803
and protect the systems that issue or approve certificates.

00:01:29.803 --> 00:01:33.188
Monitoring should identify expected endpoints, applications, users, subscriptions,

00:01:33.188 --> 00:01:36.573
and data flows without weakening encryption for convenience.

00:01:36.573 --> 00:01:40.804
When content is protected, metadata, server audit records, certificate events,

00:01:40.804 --> 00:01:43.766
and endpoint telemetry can still support investigation.

00:01:43.766 --> 00:01:48.420
Secure OPC depends on both protocol features and disciplined lifecycle management.
